  "strategy": "1.Approach and rationale  \nWe introduce a new methodology based on **evolutionary computation** combined with **local optimization** in a hierarchical framework to enhance both exploration and exploitation capabilities. Unlike traditional optimization methods that rely on local gradients, we use a population-based approach with a variable resolution strategy to navigate the complex, non-convex landscape of the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem.  \n\nThe core idea is to:  \n- Employ a **genetic algorithm (GA)** with real-valued chromosomes and a **non-dominated sorting (NSGA)** multi-objective optimizer to maintain diversity in the search population and escape local optima  \n- Implement a **resolution-hopping strategy** that dynamically evaluates functions at multiple sampling densities (n=360,480,720,960) based on error gradients and bounded validation  \n- Integrate a **gradient-based local refiner** (using smooth_max_and_grad) to polish promising candidates toward higher precision  \n- Use **constrained optimization** with performance-penalized constraints and a robust projection mechanism (project_to_sum)  \n\nThis approach addresses four key bottlenecks in previous methods:  \n1. Limited exploration radius in local optimization  \n2. Non-adaptive hyperparameter adjustment  \n3. Gradient underflow from finite-difference computation  \n4. Rigid resolution-bound selection  \n\nThe algorithm's success depends on balancing:  \n- Global exploration through crossover-mutation  \n- Dimensionality reduction via resolution adaptation  \n- Local feasibility checks with European options smoothing  \n\nExpected benefits:  \n- Jumping out of local optima by leveraging multi-resolution topologies  \n- Achieving ~0.380 majority plateau from higher dimensionality jumps  \n- Faster convergence through analytical gradient embedding  \n\n2.Mathematical specification  \nVariables:  \n- Piecewise-constant function h:[0,2]\u2192[0,1] sampled as h\u2208\u211d\u207f with sum(h)=n/2  \n- Correlation token k\u2208{-n+1,...,n-1} defines crossings h(x)\u00b7(1-h(x+k))  \n- Primary objective: min\u2096 \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx  \n- Secondary metrics: correlation spectrum gradients, constraint satisfaction  \n\nConstraints:  \n0\u2264h_i\u22641, \u2211h_i=n/2, defined as:  \nproj.Post-pruning: h\u2192h.clip(0,1)  \nproj.Sum: weighted normalized residuals  \n\nObjective decomposition:  \nC\u2085(h) = argmax\u2096 [\u2211\u1d62 h\u1d62\u00b7\u03b4\u1d62,k] / dx  \nWhere \u03b4\u1d62,k incorporates boundary effects and overlap fractions:  \n\u03b4\u1d62,k = h\u1d62\u00b7(1-h\u1d62-k) if i,n-valid  \n\u03b4\u1d62,k=0 otherwise  \n\n3.Implementation procedure  \nA. Initialization:  \n   - Generate population of 20 candidates at base resolution 360 (using:  \n      * Provided parent interpolation if available  \n      * Multi-scale sine perturbations  \n      * $\\operatorname{sinc}^{\\#}(\\cdot)$-optimized pulse templates)  \n   - Project all individuals to \u2211=n/2 via iterative residual distribution ($R \\to e^{-|R|}$)  \n   - Compute C\u2085 scores for all individuals using smooth_max_and_grad  \n\nB. Core loop:  \n   1. Evaluate fitness with NSGA-III ranking  \n   2. Pulverize population via:  \n        a. Tournament selection (tournament size=20)  \n        b. Crossover (BLX-\u03b1 with \u03b1=0.2) with dynamic step-size scaling  \n        c. Mutation with piecewise error regression:  \n            $\\Delta h_i \\sim \\epsilon\\cdot (1-\\text{sign}(\\nabla_{hi}C_5))$  \n   3. Apply PRD-inspired local refinement:  \n        For winner candidates, use smooth_max_and_grad with adaptive $\\alpha \\in [10,1000]$  \n   4. Dynamic dimensionality reduction:  \n        If correlation gradient entropy < 0.5, migrate to higher resolution  \n        Using interpolation + perturbation  \n\nC. Stopping rules:  \n   - Timekeeper: exit when budget < 75s or  \n   - Convergence: if max improvement per generation < 1e-5 for 30 generations  \n   - Feasibility window: if all individuals violate constraints, restart with $\\Delta h_i \\sim \\mathcal{U}(-0.01,0.01)$  \n\n4.Search choices  \nA. Default strategy configuration:  \n```python\nsearch_config = {\n    'cross_prob': [0.8, 0.9, 1.0],  # Adapative mean\n    'mut_rate': [0.3, 0.4, 0.5],    # Polya windowed mutation\n    'smooth_coefficients': [0.8, 1.0,  others]\n}\n```\n\nB. Pivot variations:  \n1. **Resolution-variant**: Triggers when smoothness < 0.2, switching to next resolution  \n2. **Penalty enforcement**: $(\\text{violation})^2 e^{-|\\cdot|}$  penalty scheduling  \n3. **Clipping recovery**: For boundary violations, use $\\lambda$-shrinkage:  \n   $h_i \\to h_i \\cdot \\exp(-\\max(0,|h_i-0.5|)/0.3)$  \n\n5.Compute budget and robustness  \nTime estimates:  \n- Base resolution (n=360): ~500ms/core evaluation + 300ms/mutation  \n- Estimated runs per resolution: 20\u00d71000 =200K evaluations  \n- Budget projection: 5s/base resolution, 20s/half-resolution jump  \n- Failure recovery: Gaussian process surrogate training if time <10s  \n\nA. Phases:  \nPhase_duration | Operation  \n---|---  \n0-5% | Initial expansion  \n10-40% | Intermediate refinement (crossover)  \n45-75% | High-resolution intensification  \n75-95% | Multi-resolution balancing  \n95-100% | Top candidates final polish  \n\nB. Error handling:  \n```python\ndef recover_feasible_solution(h, emp_residual):\n    # Use walking repair: \n    #   $h_i \\leftarrow \\text{clip}(h_i + \\text{Poisson}(\\max(-5,\\text{emp_residual}))$\n    #   Periodically project sum\n    pass\n```\n\n6.Validation and return contract  \nCandidates are considered valid if:  \n```python\ndef validate_candidate(h, n):\n    return (  \n        np.all((0 <= h_i <= 1 for h_i in h[...])) &  \n        abs(sum(h) - n/2) < 1e-8 &  \n        not any(np.isnan(x) for x in h)\n    )\n```\n\nPost-processing:  \n```python\ndef finalize_candidate(h, n, budget_s):\n    dx = 2.0 / n\n    c5 = compute_c5(h)  # Mode-full correlation\n    time_used = budget_s - (time.time() - start_time)\n    \n    # Sanity check correlation computation\n    corr_full = np.correlate(h, 1.0-h, mode=\"full\")\n    assert np.isclose(c5*dx*len(corr_full)/2, compute_max_overlap(h))\n    \n    return h, c5, n, time_used\n```\n\nError bounds:  \n- Theoretical $C_5 \\leq 0.3808$ is enforced via constraint  \n- Computed value $(C_5)$ is the average over all Lag strikes:  \n  $C_5 = \\frac{1}{|\\text{valid}_\\text{Lag}|} \\sum_k \\max_k \\text{cumulative}_{\\text{overlap}}$\n\nPseudocode summary:\noverview_diagram = ChainMap(\n    'Genetic_algorithm',\n    'NSGA-III_selection',\n    'Multi-resolution_boost',\n    'Constraint_recovery',\n    'Gradient_embedding'\n)",
